\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces Optical layout of the $461~\text {nm}$ blue laser system for transverse cooling, Zeeman slowing, and blue MOT.}}{8}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces Adapted from P.Bataille thesis \cite {Bataille2020} Diagram of the frequencies of each laser composing the blue chain of the blue MOT broadband cooling. Blue dashed lines indicate the tunable frequencies of the lasers. Plain black lines indicate the transitions of \text {$^{87}Sr$ and $^{88}Sr$ isotopes.}}}{9}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the transverse cooling effec on the atoms arriving from the oven spread in different velocity classes. }}{10}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.7}{\ignorespaces Transverse cooling performance as a function of laser detuning and power (adapted from \cite {faisant_building_2024}).}}{11}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.8}{\ignorespaces Energy level diagram of $^{87}\text {Sr}$ of the transition $^3P_2$ to the hyperfine states of the $^3D_J$ at $403~\text {nm}$ cooling.}}{14}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.9}{\ignorespaces Optical setup for the repump and the beat-lock system.}}{14}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.10}{\ignorespaces Optical setup of the $689~\text {nm}$ laser cooling system.}}{15}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.11}{\ignorespaces Frequencies of the red lasers used for the red MOT cooling stages and optical pumping. The red dashed lines correspond to the tunable frequencies, the dark plain lines for the $^{87}Sr$ hyperfine states of transition $^3P_J$ and the $^{88}Sr$}}{16}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.12}{\ignorespaces Counterpropagating lasers in resonance with lightshifted $m_f$ states of $^3P_1, F=11/2$ hyperfine state.}}{17}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.13}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the melange concept added to the red MOT cooling to capture extreme spin states components unaccessible from the counterpropagating lasers of the red MOT.}}{18}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.14}{\ignorespaces Sequential stages of the $689~\text {nm}$ red MOT. From left to right : swap cooling and broadband capture (adapted from \cite {StellmerThesis}). The size of the cloud is decreasing with the cooling since the atoms are kicked in the opposite direction of the resonant laser of right polarization.}}{19}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.15}{\ignorespaces Simulation extracted from \cite {litvinov_these}. Principle of localized $689~\text {nm}$ MOT loading inside the crossed optical dipole trap due to the differential AC Stark shift between $^1S_0$ and $^3P_1$.}}{20}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.16}{\ignorespaces Evaporative cooling mechanism in a crossed optical dipole trap, illustrating the escape of high-energy atoms and subsequent re-thermalization.}}{21}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.17}{\ignorespaces Optical setup of the $1064~\text {nm}$ crossed dipole trap laser system.}}{22}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.18}{\ignorespaces Optical pumping scheme on the $^1S_0 (F=9/2) \rightarrow {}^3P_1 (F=11/2)$ transition used to prepare a specific nuclear spin mixture. The red laser is ramped through the target energy level of $^3P_1$: the atoms decay to neighbours states $m_F \pm 1$ of $^1S_0$}}{23}{figure.caption.59}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.19}{\ignorespaces MOGlabs External Cavity Diode Laser (ECDL) architecture used for spin manipulation. A diode lasing at 689~nm is frequency settled by an interferential filter marked in red to then be collimated by a lens. The beam is reflected on a second miroir to close a cavity acting as a frequency filter: this system is a cateye. The last lens is used to collimate the beam passing throught an isolater before leaving the laser head. }}{24}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.20}{\ignorespaces Spin manipulation scheme of \cite {bataille_realisation_nodate}. The isolation of spin states is allowed by the tensorial shift of the ground states that makes neighbours $m_F$ states isolated from the other resonances.}}{25}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.21}{\ignorespaces Optical layout of the MOGlabs laser chain configured for two-photon Raman transitions.}}{26}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.22}{\ignorespaces Complete optical setup including the Fabry-Pérot cavity and PDH stabilization loop.}}{27}{figure.caption.72}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.23}{\ignorespaces Top : Initial fixed cavity assembly using glued mirrors, lacking mechanical alignment degrees of freedom. Bottom : Upgraded and final cavity mount featuring three adjustment screws and compliant washers for precise angular and longitudinal alignment}}{28}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.24}{\ignorespaces Schematic of the Pound-Drever-Hall locking system, extracted and adapted from \cite {black_Gas_preparation_and_spin_control_2001}. The laser is firstly passing through an EOM that is modulating the eletric field at frequency $f_M$, creating two sidebands at frequency $f_{laser}\pm f_M$. The carrier at $f_{laser}$ and the sidebands are passing through the filtering Fabry-Pérot cavity wich is reflecting the light going back to the entrance of the cavity. A beamsplitter serving as an optical isolator is reflecting the light to a photodiode. Then this signal is mixed with a local oscillator to extract an error signal to which a low-pass filter is combined to keep only the signal for the locking.}}{29}{figure.caption.81}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.25}{\ignorespaces Pound-Drever-Hall (PDH) error signal obtained by scanning the cavity length across resonance, enabling the lock cavity for the filtering of MOGlabs laser.}}{30}{figure.caption.82}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.26}{\ignorespaces Homemade PID scheme composed by the separated proportionnal and integrated part we considered sufficiant for our setup.}}{31}{figure.caption.83}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.27}{\ignorespaces Closed-loop noise analysis and mechanical resonance identification using the Red Pitaya with a gain that is amplifying parasit noise, such as the mechanical resonance of the piezo at $f = 35~$kHz.}}{31}{figure.caption.84}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.28}{\ignorespaces Principle of spin-selective detection via coherent momentum transfer. (a) Counter-propagating beam geometry of the lasers used for the TLS and two-photon Raman scheme. (b) Energy levels scheme of the Raman process, with recoil energy shifting the $m_F+2$ level. (c) Avoided crossing due to the coupling of the states, necessiting adiabatic transfer. (d) Resulting TOF absorption image showing spatially separated spin components and the corresponding lilentul distribution.}}{33}{figure.caption.90}%
